contact time from before we were the\n\t\t// leader. Otherwise, to a client it would seem our data\n\t\t// is extremely stale.\n\t\tr.setLastContact()\n\n\t\t// Stop replication\n\t\tfor _, p := range r.leaderState.replState {\n\t\t\tclose(p.stopCh)\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t// Respond to all inflight operations\n\t\tfor e := r.leaderState.inflight.Front(); e != nil; e = e.Next() {\n\t\t\te.Value.(*logFuture).respond(ErrLeadershipLost)\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t// Respond to any pending verify requests\n\t\tfor future := range r.leaderState.notify {\n\t\t\tfuture.respond(ErrLeadershipLost)\n\t\t}\n\n\t\t// Clear all the state\n\t\tr.leaderState.commitCh = nil\n\t\tr.leaderState.commitment = nil\n\t\tr.leaderState.inflight = nil\n\t\tr.leaderState.replState = nil\n\t\tr.leaderState.notify = nil\n\t\tr.leaderState.stepDown = nil\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run; python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor i in s['interpretations']:\n    if i['id']=='83d9dac982df44119d91328717344439-interpretation-0':\n        for k in ('source_ids','executions','joint_history','rationale','conclusion','defect_claim','counterevidence','limitations'):\n            print('###',k); print(json.dumps(i.get(k)))\n\\\" \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run; python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor i in s['interpretations']:\n    if i['id']=='83d9dac982df44119d91328717344439-interpretation-0':\n        for k in ('source_ids','executions','joint_history','rationale','conclusion','defect_claim','counterevidence','limitations'):\n            print('###',k); print(json.dumps(i.get(k)))\n\\\" \"","aggregated_output":"### source_ids\n[\"s-verifyleader\", \"s-replstate\", \"s-heartbeat\", \"s-lease\", \"s-quorum\", \"s-verifyleader-doc\", \"s-verifyleader-tests\"]\n### executions\n{\"7401ab5a8f7c4cd69d947e972ea31a61\": [22, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27], \"c0f7723f05d8430aaf36bf2b4f9ce862\": [22, 23, 24, 25], \"5e092a2b160b4a2f955d48ee6fa06ad9\": [22, 23]}\n### joint_history\n\"Three receipts answer this question. The first (5e092a2b160b4a2f955d48ee6fa06ad9 at indices 22 and 23) measured the isolation with slowed timers; the second (c0f7723f05d8430aaf36bf2b4f9ce862 at 22-25) ran it with slowed and default timers; the third (7401ab5a8f7c4cd69d947e972ea31a61 at 22-27) repeats both cases and measures when the isolated leader stops reporting itself as leader.\"\n### rationale\n\"Current account: leader verification can be satisfied without a voter quorum, and the resulting incorrect answer is available for about one lease interval before the same node stops leading. Evidence: in the latest receipt the slowed-timer setup event 22 records three voters with a quorum of two, a voter unreachable by direct AppendEntries and a reachable non-voter, and its result event 23 records verification succeeding with no error in under a millisecond while the node still led; events 25 and 26 repeat the premise and the same result with the repository's default in-memory timers; the bound events 24 and 27 record the node becoming a follower after 1012 ms with a 1000 ms lease and after 60 ms with the default 50 ms lease. Mechanism from source: verifyLeader starts its count at one for itself and registers the future in every entry of the leader's replication map, which startStopReplication builds for every server of the configuration except the leader without filtering by suffrage; each peer's heartbeat acknowledges the pending verification on a successful AppendEntries, so a non-voter's acknowledgement raises the count to the voter quorum computed from voters alone, while the lease check counts voters only and therefore closes the window about one lease interval later. The documented purpose of the call, anchored, is to ensure the peer is still the leader and to prevent returning stale data from the FSM after leadership is lost, which is what a non-voter acknowledgement cannot establish. Coverage: the captured tests that exercise this call use voter-only clusters, so nothing in the snapshot pins the non-voter path either way.\"\n### conclusion\n\"violated\"\n### defect_claim\n\"asserted\"\n### counterevidence\n[\"The measured bound limits the impact: the node stops reporting itself as leader about one lease interval after losing the voter quorum, so the incorrect verification result is available only inside that interval.\", \"The defect requires a configuration containing non-voters; in a voters-only cluster every replication peer is a voter and the acknowledgement set equals the voter set, so the path cannot diverge from the voter quorum. The captured verification tests are exactly that shape: a three-voter cluster with one follower disconnected expects success because a voter quorum remains reachable, and a forced higher term on a follower expects ErrNotLeader or ErrLeadershipLost.\", \"No write path is affected: entry commitment uses the commitment map, which contains voters only, so this is a read-verification path rather than a commit-rule path.\", \"The probe calls VerifyLeader directly rather than through an application that then serves a read, so the stale-read consequence is argued from the anchored documentation rather than measured end to end.\"]\n### limitations\n[\"Measured on four-peer in-memory clusters with the repository's own facilities; production timings and transports are not covered.\", \"The bound is reported for the two timer configurations used (1000 ms and the default 50 ms lease) and depends on the lease timeout, not on a fixed wall-clock constant.\", \"Both cases isolate the leader by transport disconnection and confirm it with direct AppendEntries attempts recorded in the same events.\"]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run/agent-source; sed -n '29,52p' commitment.go\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run/agent-source; sed -n '29,52p' commitment.go\"","aggregated_output":"// newCommitment returns a commitment struct that notifies the provided\n// channel when log entries have been committed. A new commitment struct is\n// created each time this server becomes leader for a particular term.\n// 'configuration' is the servers in the cluster.\n// 'startIndex' is the first index created in this term (see\n// its description above).\nfunc newCommitment(commitCh chan struct{}, configuration Configuration, startIndex uint64) *commitment {\n\tmatchIndexes := make(map[ServerID]uint64)\n\tfor _, server := range configuration.Servers {\n\t\tif server.Suffrage == Voter {\n\t\t\tmatchIndexes[server.ID] = 0\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn &commitment{\n\t\tcommitCh:     commitCh,\n\t\tmatchIndexes: matchIndexes,\n\t\tcommitIndex:  0,\n\t\tstartIndex:   startIndex,\n\t}\n}\n\n// Called when a new cluster membership configuration is created: it will be\n// used to determine commitment from now on. 'configuration' is the servers in\n// the cluster.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && mv update.json update.prev.json && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nu={\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Source-derived breadth check on the point left open for the leader-verification question, whether any other consumer of peer acknowledgements treats non-voters as though they counted. Reading the captured tree: the commitment map is built from the configuration's voters only and its match() ignores any server absent from that map, so a non-voter's replication acknowledgement cannot advance the commit index; the lease check filters on suffrage before counting contact; quorumSize counts voters only. That leaves verifyLeader as the only place a pending check is registered against every entry of the replication map, so the open point narrows from unchecked to surveyed with a single consumer. Recorded as source-derived evidence reusing the existing receipts, not as a new measurement; no execution is requested.\\\",\n \\\"sources\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"s-commitment-map\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"agent-source/commitment.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":29,\\\"end_line\\\":46,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"}],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[{\n  \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"790eb5d64ed94ef5aea4736e6f66381d-question-0\\\",\n  \\\"experiment_id\\\":\\\"f6dbda4bb1ee4baca7e8f055f109412f-experiment\\\",\n  \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"s-verifyleader\\\",\\\"s-replstate\\\",\\\"s-heartbeat\\\",\\\"s-lease\\\",\\\"s-quorum\\\",\\\"s-verifyleader-doc\\\",\\\"s-verifyleader-tests\\\",\\\"s-commitment-map\\\"],\n  \\\"executions\\\":{\\\"7401ab5a8f7c4cd69d947e972ea31a61\\\":[22,23,24,25,26,27],\\\"c0f7723f05d8430aaf36bf2b4f9ce862\\\":[22,23,24,25],\\\"5e092a2b160b4a2f955d48ee6fa06ad9\\\":[22,23]},\n  \\\"joint_history\\\":\\\"Three receipts answer this question. The first (5e092a2b160b4a2f955d48ee6fa06ad9 at indices 22 and 23) measured the isolation with slowed timers; the second (c0f7723f05d8430aaf36bf2b4f9ce862 at 22-25) ran it with slowed and default timers; the third (7401ab5a8f7c4cd69d947e972ea31a61 at 22-27) repeats both cases and measures when the isolated leader stops reporting itself as leader.\\\",\n  \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Current account: leader verification can be satisfied without a voter quorum, and the resulting incorrect answer is available for about one lease interval before the same node stops leading. Evidence: in the latest receipt the slowed-timer setup event 22 records three voters with a quorum of two, a voter unreachable by direct AppendEntries and a reachable non-voter, and its result event 23 records verification succeeding with no error in under a millisecond while the node still led; events 25 and 26 repeat the premise and the same result with the repository's default in-memory timers; the bound events 24 and 27 record the node becoming a follower after 1012 ms with a 1000 ms lease and after 60 ms with the default 50 ms lease. Mechanism from source: verifyLeader starts its count at one for itself and registers the future in every entry of the leader's replication map, which startStopReplication builds for every server of the configuration except the leader without filtering by suffrage; each peer's heartbeat acknowledges the pending verification on a successful AppendEntries, so a non-voter's acknowledgement raises the count to the voter quorum computed from voters alone, while the lease check counts voters only and therefore closes the window about one lease interval later. The documented purpose of the call, anchored, is to ensure the peer is still the leader and to prevent returning stale data from the FSM after leadership is lost, which is what a non-voter acknowledgement cannot establish. Breadth of the path, newly anchored: the commitment map that decides entry commitment is constructed from the configuration's voters only and its match() ignores a server that is not already a key of that map, so a non-voter's acknowledgement cannot advance the commit index; the lease check filters on suffrage before counting; quorumSize counts voters only. verifyLeader is therefore the single consumer of peer acknowledgements that admits a non-voter, and no second consumer of that kind was found in the captured tree. Coverage: the captured tests that exercise this call use voter-only clusters, so nothing in the snapshot pins the non-voter path either way.\\\",\n  \\\"conclusion\\\":\\\"violated\\\",\n  \\\"defect_claim\\\":\\\"asserted\\\",\n  \\\"counterevidence\\\":[\n   \\\"The measured bound limits the impact: the node stops reporting itself as leader about one lease interval after losing the voter quorum, so the incorrect verification result is available only inside that interval.\\\",\n   \\\"The defect requires a configuration containing non-voters; in a voters-only cluster every replication peer is a voter and the acknowledgement set equals the voter set, so the path cannot diverge from the voter quorum. The captured verification tests are exactly that shape: a three-voter cluster with one follower disconnected expects success because a voter quorum remains reachable, and a forced higher term on a follower expects ErrNotLeader or ErrLeadershipLost.\\\",\n   \\\"No write path is affected: entry commitment uses the commitment map, whose keys are the configuration's voters and whose match() ignores any other server, so this is a read-verification path rather than a commit-rule path.\\\",\n   \\\"The probe calls VerifyLeader directly rather than through an application that then serves a read, so the stale-read consequence is argued from the anchored documentation rather than measured end to end.\\\"],\n  \\\"limitations\\\":[\n   \\\"Measured on four-peer in-memory clusters with the repository's own facilities; production timings and transports are not covered.\\\",\n   \\\"The bound is reported for the two timer configurations used (1000 ms and the default 50 ms lease) and depends on the lease timeout, not on a fixed wall-clock constant.\\\",\n   \\\"Both cases isolate the leader by transport disconnection and confirm it with direct AppendEntries attempts recorded in the same events.\\\",\n   \\\"The breadth statement is source-derived and limited to the captured tree, and every execution in the record concerns the verification path itself: the finding that the commitment map and the lease check exclude non-voters is read from the anchored construction and check, not measured, so a consumer outside the captured tree would not appear here.\\\"]\n }]}\nopen('update.json','w').write(json.dumps(u,indent=2)+\\\"\\\\n\\\")\nprint(\\\"written\\\")\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
