{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_18-00-28-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nnew=json.load(open('update.json'))\nprev=json.load(open('update.prev.json'))\nnew['rationale']=(\\\"Two additive changes in one update. First, the carried-over account for the empty-configuration question, which was drafted but not yet applied: with an empty recovered configuration the node admits any transport-reachable sender on votes and on replication, and a live cluster bounds the effect because appendEntries records whatever sender arrives as leader while the follower loop clears a known leader only when contact stops, so the restarted node converges back on a heartbeating legitimate leader. That statement is source-derived from the cited ranges, not measured in a multi-node probe. Second, a source-derived breadth check on the point left open for the leader-verification question, whether any other consumer of peer acknowledgements treats non-voters as though they counted: the commitment map is built from the configuration's voters only and its match() ignores any server absent from that map, so a non-voter's replication acknowledgement cannot advance the commit index; the lease check filters on suffrage before counting contact; quorumSize counts voters only. That leaves verifyLeader as the only place a pending check is registered against every entry of the replication map, so the open point narrows from unchecked to surveyed with a single consumer. No execution is requested.\\\")\nnew['interpretations']=prev['interpretations']+new['interpretations']\nopen('update.json','w').write(json.dumps(new,indent=2)+\\\"\\\\n\\\")\nprint(len(new['interpretations']))\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli 